<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>{% if title %}{{ title }} — Largs{% else %}{{ site.title }}{% endif %}</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Marcellus&family=Alegreya+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,700;1,400&family=Spline+Sans+Mono:wght@400;500;600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
{# Fingerprinted: the filename carries a hash of the file's contents, so
   a change is always a new URL and no cache can serve a stale copy. See
   the cssHref block in eleventy.config.js for what went wrong without it. #}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ cssHref }}">
{# Canonical. The site answers on largs.scot, www.largs.scot and the
   pages.dev deployment address, all serving identical pages -- without
   this, that is three copies of every page as far as a search engine is
   concerned. site.url is the single place the canonical host is set. #}
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">

{# ---- What search engines and shared links see -------------------------
   The description falls back through: an explicit `description` in a
   page's front matter, then its `subtitle`, then the site default. Every
   page already carries a subtitle written for humans -- "Where to take a
   fault so it reaches the people who can fix it" -- which is a better
   search snippet than anything written for a search engine, so it is
   reused rather than duplicated. Set `description` in front matter only
   where a page wants different words in results than on the page. #}
{%- set metaTitle = title + " — Largs" if title else site.title -%}
{%- set metaDesc = description or subtitle or site.description -%}
<meta name="description" content="{{ metaDesc }}">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#17333A">

{# The longship: the Battle of Largs, and the town's own emblem. Cream on
   slate to match the masthead, and because a pale tile loses its edges
   against a light browser toolbar at 16px. #}
<link rel="icon" href="/assets/img/favicon-32.png" sizes="32x32" type="image/png">
<link rel="icon" href="/assets/img/favicon-16.png" sizes="16x16" type="image/png">
<link rel="icon" href="/assets/img/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/img/apple-touch-icon.png">
<link rel="manifest" href="/site.webmanifest">

{# Open Graph: what a shared link looks like in a Facebook or WhatsApp
   post, which is how most of Largs will meet this site. og:image is
   deliberately conditional -- an og:image pointing at nothing is worse
   than none at all, so the card stays a plain link until `ogImage` is
   set in site.json and the artwork exists. #}
<meta property="og:site_name" content="largs.scot">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ metaDesc }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta property="og:locale" content="en_GB">
{%- if site.ogImage %}
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ site.url }}{{ site.ogImage }}">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
{%- else %}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
{%- endif %}

{# Tells Google the site's name, so results read "largs.scot" rather than
   a guess made from the domain. #}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebSite","name":"largs.scot","alternateName":"Largs — …for you","url":"{{ site.url }}"}
</script>
</head>
<body>

<header class="masthead">
  {# Magnus — the Viking statue on the seafront, rendered as cream
     linework so the banner shows through him, the same treatment as
     the horizon silhouette below. Decorative (alt empty) so screen
     readers are not told about him on every page. Generated from a
     photograph of the statue; sculpture permanently sited in a public
     place (CDPA s62). TODO: confirm the sculptor's name and credit
     them in the colophon. #}
  <img class="masthead-magnus" src="/assets/img/magnus.png"
       alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="320" height="640"
       loading="eager" decoding="async">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="masthead-inner">
      <div>
        <a class="wordmark" href="/">Largs</a>
        <div class="tagline">…for you</div>
      </div>
      <div class="dateline">{{ buildTime | dateLong }} · Firth of Clyde</div>
    </div>
    <nav class="mainnav" aria-label="Main">
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/"{% if page.url == "/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Today</a></li>
        <li><a href="/whats-on/"{% if page.url == "/whats-on/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>What's on</a></li>
        <li><a href="/bins/"{% if page.url == "/bins/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Bin days</a></li>
        <li><a href="/roadworks/"{% if page.url and "/roadworks/" in page.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Roadworks</a></li>
        <li><a href="/council/"{% if page.url == "/council/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Largs Community Council</a></li>
        <li><a href="/on-the-agenda/"{% if page.url and "/on-the-agenda/" in page.url %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>North Ayrshire Council</a></li>
        <li><a href="/photos/"{% if page.url == "/photos/" %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Photos</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

{# Signature: the Largs horizon — Cumbrae, a CalMac ferry mid-crossing, and the Pencil.

   THE FERRY AND THE PENCIL ARE NOT IN THE SVG, and that is the whole point.
   The band stretches to the window width with preserveAspectRatio="none",
   which is right for hills — a hill can be any shape — and wrong for a
   drawn object: on a wide screen the ferry would be stretched by a third
   and read as a barge. So the background stretches and the two objects sit
   over it as fixed-aspect images, bottom-aligned to the waterline.

   Both are drawn artwork rather than rectangles now, which is why the band
   grew from 64px to 80px: at 64 the Pencil's arched door and the ferry's
   mast and ramps disappeared, and without the door the tower reads as a
   post rather than a building.

   The aria-label describes the whole scene; the images themselves are
   decorative and carry empty alt, so a screen reader hears the horizon
   described once rather than three times. #}
<div class="horizon" role="img" aria-label="Silhouette of the Largs horizon: Great Cumbrae across the water, the ferry mid-crossing, and the Pencil monument on the shore">
  <svg class="horizon-bg" viewBox="0 0 1200 80" preserveAspectRatio="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
    <rect width="1200" height="80" fill="#BFDED2"/>
    <rect y="57.5" width="1200" height="22.5" fill="#17333A"/>
    <path d="M0 57.5 L120 57.5 Q210 22.5 320 42.5 Q420 57.5 520 57.5 Z" fill="#17333A"/>
    <path d="M300 57.5 Q400 32.5 500 50 Q560 57.5 620 57.5 Z" fill="#17333A" opacity="0.55"/>
  </svg>
  <img class="horizon-obj horizon-ferry" src="/assets/img/horizon-ferry.png"
       alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="1026" height="420" loading="eager" decoding="async">
  <img class="horizon-obj horizon-pencil" src="/assets/img/horizon-pencil.png"
       alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="286" height="842" loading="eager" decoding="async">
</div>

<main>
{# Every page names itself once, in plain English, at a size the whole
   town can read. Set `heading` and `subtitle` in a page's front matter;
   the spaced-uppercase kicker below it is a secondary label only. #}
{% if heading %}
<div class="wrap page-head">
  <h1 class="page-title" id="page-title">{{ heading }}</h1>
  {% if subtitle %}<p class="page-subtitle">{{ subtitle }}</p>{% endif %}
</div>
{% endif %}
{{ content | safe }}
</main>

<footer>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="foot">
      <div class="founder">
        <h3>Built for the town</h3>
        <p>A community reference site for Largs — the day's essentials, what's
        on, and council business in plain English. Every figure on these pages
        is fetched live, sourced to a public record, or labelled honestly while
        the real thing is wired up. Free to use, run by a local volunteer. New sections arrive as they're ready.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="foot-meta">
        <h4>About this site</h4>
        <p>Community-run, for the town, free to use.<br>
        <a href="/council/">Largs Community Council</a> · <a href="/on-the-agenda/">North Ayrshire Council</a> · <a href="/roadworks/">Roadworks</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="colophon">
      <span><a href="/">largs.scot</a> · …for you</span>
      {# Attribution follows the tide source. UKHO's reply may specify
         exact wording — if it does, update the first branch to match. #}
      {% if today.tides.source == "ukho" %}
      <span>Weather data © <a href="https://open-meteo.com">Open-Meteo.com</a> (CC BY 4.0) · Tidal predictions (Millport station): ADMIRALTY® UK Tidal API © Crown copyright</span>
      {% else %}
      <span>Weather &amp; tide model data © <a href="https://open-meteo.com">Open-Meteo.com</a> (CC BY 4.0)</span>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
</footer>

</body>
</html>
